window.docContents[6].resolve({"/ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation/Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-widest-collisions-in-the-series/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation--Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-widest-collisions-in-the-series":{"contents":"ISO 80000-7 holds the widest dimension collisions of the catalogue. The candela reducing\nto power and the steradian to one, radiant flux, luminous flux, radiant intensity, and\nluminous intensity all share M·L²·T⁻³; radiance, luminance, irradiance, illuminance,\nand the exitances all share M·T⁻³; and over two dozen kinds — refractive index, the\nefficiencies and efficacies, emissivity, the absorptances, reflectances, and\ntransmittances, the optical densities, the radiance and luminance factors, the\nchromaticity coordinates, the colour-matching functions, the photon number — share\ndimension one.\n\n\n\nRadiant flux is not luminous flux, though both are M·L²·T⁻³. There exist distinct\nISO 80000-7 kinds with the same dimension — radiant and luminous flux witness it, alongside\nradiant and luminous intensity. The candela reduces to power, so no dimension-only type\nsystem can separate them; the kind layer — and the unit, W versus lm — does. This is a\ncollision between a base-quantity unit and a derived one, the scale-spanning case (R13)\non the standard. Uses the dimension map.\n\niso80000_7_dim_collision : ∃ a b : DimensionedKind, a.kind ≠ b.kind ∧ a.dim = b.dim,\nwitnessed by ⟨radiantFlux, luminousFlux, …⟩. The companion\nradiantFlux_dim_eq_radiantIntensity_dim is the steradian collision (flux versus\nintensity, the steradian being dimension one), and\nwattRadiant_lumen_not_commensurable / wattRadiant_wattPerSteradian_not_commensurable\nrecord the unit-level facts. Axiom-free.\n\nThe dimension-1 disambiguation, on the standard — the widest in the series. There exist\ndistinct ISO 80000-7 kinds with the same dimension one — refractive index and emissivity\nwitness it, alongside the efficiencies, efficacies, absorptances, reflectances,\ntransmittances, optical densities, the radiance and luminance factors, the reflectance\nfactor, the chromaticity coordinates, the colour-matching functions, and the photon number.\nDimension cannot separate them; the kind layer does. Uses the dimension\nmap.\n\niso80000_7_dim_one_collision : ∃ a b : DimensionedKind, a.kind ≠ b.kind ∧ a.dim = b.dim ∧\na.dim = 1, witnessed by ⟨refractiveIndex, emissivity, …⟩ with refractiveIndex_ne_emissivity\n(a decide) and reflexivity. Axiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-7 — Light and radiation","header":"25.3. Dimension does not classify; the kind does — the widest collisions in the series","id":"/ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation/Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-widest-collisions-in-the-series/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation--Dimension-does-not-classify_SEMI_-the-kind-does-___-the-widest-collisions-in-the-series"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/#hcp":{"contents":"This part is for a PhysLib contributor: a physicist who writes Lean in PhysLib and\nalready knows Lean, PhysLib, Dimension, and WithDim. The method is top-down refinement:\nbegin with the broad system context, then state the attributes of interest, interleaving\ncontext and attribute refinement as new subsystems or phenomena enter the model.\n\nLowe's four-category square separates substantial things from ways things are, and\nuniversals from particulars:\n\n* Axis* Substantial things* Non-substantial ways things are\n* Universal* Sort of system — SortOfSystem* Kind of property — KindOfProperty\n* Particular* System or object — index o* Individual quantity — IndividualQuantity o k R\n\nThe left column is system context; dimension-only metrology attends to the right.\n\nThe ladder starts with one black-box oscillator and adds a second oscillator as another\nparticular of the same sort. L2 opens each box into body and spring(s), then treats the\npair as a whole. Later steps open the body to its mass distribution, move out of the line,\noptionally add damping, and finally state what must hold:\n\n* Ladder step* Refinement* Context change* New attributes* What goes wrong* PhysLib stage/source* Status\n* L0 — The black box* One homogeneous, rigid, point-like body on one ideal spring to a wall, along a line* One system; body and spring inside, wall outside; the wall is fixed and infinitely massive* Mass, stiffness, displacement, ω vs f, period* One oscillator. Bare reals accept the inverted period formula √(m/k); WithDim refuses that but accepts angular frequency ω plus frequency f, which share dimension T⁻¹. Only distinct kinds refuse both.* Stages 0–1; upstream oscillator Basic and WithDim; ForPhysLib Kinds and Metrology; Exhibit C* Included here\n* L1 — Two bodies* One body inside each black-box oscillator* Two particulars of sort harmonic oscillator; each black box contains one body and its wall spring; the oscillators remain uncoupled* Same kinds, now indexed by the oscillator whole; its mass and stiffness share an index* Two separate oscillators, A and B. Cross-system contamination: with kinds but no bearer, B's mass can be used in A's frequency, because the kind is right. False identity: giving both oscillators one name makes them one system. Indexing each quantity by its oscillator refuses the first; an injective designation the second.* Stage 2; CaseStudies/HarmonicOscillator Attempt 4; HCP L1* Included here\n* L2 — The pair is a system too* The pair becomes a system in its own right* Opens each oscillator into body and spring(s); pair's own sort, whole and parts; coupling, parallel and series springs* Normal-mode frequency ω₊, total mass, effective stiffness* One coupled pair, opened into bodies A and B and springs kA, kC, kB. Aggregating what does not aggregate: the pair's mass is mA + mB, but its normal-mode frequency ω₊ is not ωA + ωB. Wrong composition law: the licence to add masses belongs to the sort 'coupled pair', not to the kind 'mass'; stiffnesses add in parallel but not in series.* Stage 3; HCP pairMass/parallelStiffness, Composition finding 4* Included here\n* L3 — The body opens up* Body A's centre of mass is off-axis, producing torque and wobble* Body A of pair P becomes a nominally indexed rigid composite with left/right halves; a body-fixed frame enters beside the lab frame* Per-half mass and position, centre of mass, inertia tensor, frame-specific angular velocity* Body A opened into two halves. Wrong carving: counting a half twice or leaving one out gives another mass. Weighted mean vs sum: the centre of mass is the mass-weighted mean of the halves' positions, and ω is the same for every half. Degenerate whole: a massless body's centre of mass evaluates to the origin, because Lean defines 1/0 = 0. Frames: the lab-frame ω put into the body-frame energy type-checks in PhysLib.* Stages 2–3 on RigidBody; Exhibits RigidBody and Composition; upstream CM-RB-sphereI informal lemma proved in SolidSphereInertia.lean* Included here\n* L4 — Out of the line* Motion in three dimensions, seen by a moving observer* Motion in 3-D, seen from the lab and a moving cart* Position vector, displacement, distance, velocity per frame* Pair P in 3-D, seen from the lab and a moving cart. Bearer erased: distanceQ cannot tell P's separation from Q's. Observer left implicit: one bare vector type accepts all three frame-conversion laws (displacement, velocity, relative position), though only one is right for each kind of vector.* Stages 0–4 on Space; Exhibit ReferenceFrame* Included here\n* L5 — The oscillation dies out* Add a damper (optional)* Add a damper to the nominally named oscillator* Damper γ, whole-system decay rate δ, and damping regime* Oscillator A with a damper. PhysLib's γ (kg/s), ISO's damping coefficient δ = γ/(2m) (s⁻¹), and ω₀ (rad/s) share symbols or dimensions; bare reals accept γ < ω₀ and dimensions accept δ + ω₀.* Stage 1; upstream DampedHarmonicOscillator; ISO 80000-3 items 3-18 and 3-24* Included here (optional)\n* L6 — What must hold* What must hold* Requirement scope over refined context* Requirement, referent, gate* Requirements over pair P and the sort 'coupled pair'. Unstatable scope: without named sorts and particulars, 'every pair' and 'pair P' have nothing to quantify over; a scope naming a bare number is rejected.* Stage 4; Tutorial.HarmonicOscillatorRequirements, Audits, checked_by* Included here\n\nEvery lesson line is quoted live from the HCP library through SubVerso anchors; the\nblueprint build refuses drift. L1–L6 are included here, with L5 optional; the Tutorial\nlibrary remains the model-writer's separate track.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"5. Onboarding: Human Context Protocols","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/#hcp"}});